WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.288 --> 00:00:18.918
The sun had been shining on the secret garden for nearly a week.

2
00:00:20.079 --> 00:00:24.662
Mary was beginning to like being outdoors, and she was even starting to like the wind.

3
00:00:25.923 --> 00:00:29.646
She could run faster and longer, and she could skip up to a hundred.

4
00:00:30.827 --> 00:00:35.898
Mary was a determined little person, and now that she had something to be interested in, she was

5
00:00:35.998 --> 00:00:36.839
very determined.

6
00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:41.462
She worked hard in the garden, digging and pulling up weeds.

7
00:00:42.703 --> 00:00:45.145
Instead of getting tired, she felt happy.

8
00:00:46.385 --> 00:00:51.169
During that week of sunshine, Mary met Ben Weatherstaff outdoors several times.

9
00:00:52.510 --> 00:00:57.653
She always approached quietly because she was afraid he might pick up his tools and leave if he

10
00:00:57.693 --> 00:00:58.114
saw her.

11
00:00:58.134 --> 00:01:04.973
"You are like the robin," he said to her one morning when she suddenly appeared at his side.

12
00:01:04.993 --> 00:01:07.714
"I never know when I will see you."

13
00:01:07.734 --> 00:01:11.615
"The robin is friends with me now," said Mary.

14
00:01:12.615 --> 00:01:15.636
Ben stopped working and rested one foot on his shovel.

15
00:01:15.656 --> 00:01:17.637
"How long have you lived here?"

16
00:01:17.657 --> 00:01:20.617
"I think it's been about a month."

17
00:01:20.637 --> 00:01:22.898
"You're starting to look better.

18
00:01:23.838 --> 00:01:26.599
Your face is not so pale, and you look fatter."

19
00:01:26.619 --> 00:01:29.800
"I know I'm fatter," said Mary.

20
00:01:30.728 --> 00:01:32.149
"My stockings are getting tighter.

21
00:01:32.630 --> 00:01:34.091
Oh, look, there's the robin!"

22
00:01:35.352 --> 00:01:37.734
The robin hopped closer and closer to Ben.

23
00:01:38.975 --> 00:01:43.459
He spread his wings, flew up in the air, and landed on the handle of Ben's shovel.

24
00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:46.741
Ben looked as if he didn't dare to breathe.

25
00:01:48.042 --> 00:01:50.665
Then the robin flew away, and Ben took a breath.

26
00:01:51.846 --> 00:01:54.628
There was a smile on his face as he went back to digging.

27
00:01:56.389 --> 00:01:59.192
Ben's smile gave Mary the courage to ask a question.

28
00:02:00.492 --> 00:02:02.153
"Do you have a garden of your own?"

29
00:02:03.615 --> 00:02:04.515
Ben shook his head.

30
00:02:05.656 --> 00:02:08.759
"But if you did, what kind of flowers would you plant?"

31
00:02:08.779 --> 00:02:11.981
"Bulbs, but mostly roses.

32
00:02:13.062 --> 00:02:15.584
I was a gardener for a young lady who loved roses.

33
00:02:16.825 --> 00:02:18.887
She had a lot in a place she was fond of.

34
00:02:20.348 --> 00:02:22.209
I even saw her bend over and kiss them."

35
00:02:23.350 --> 00:02:25.672
He pulled out a weed and frowned at it.

36
00:02:25.692 --> 00:02:28.454
"But that was ten years ago."

37
00:02:29.055 --> 00:02:30.016
"Where is she now?"

38
00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:34.142
"Heaven." "And what happened to her roses?"

39
00:02:34.682 --> 00:02:35.342
asked Mary.

40
00:02:35.362 --> 00:02:36.903
"Did they die too?"

41
00:02:36.923 --> 00:02:40.785
"Once or twice a year, I'd go and work at them.

42
00:02:40.845 --> 00:02:43.007
I'd prune them and dig around their roots.

43
00:02:44.147 --> 00:02:45.288
But I haven't gone this year.

44
00:02:45.728 --> 00:02:47.369
I'm too old to do all that work."

45
00:02:48.490 --> 00:02:50.491
Mary couldn't stop asking questions.

46
00:02:50.511 --> 00:02:55.533
"When the roses have no leaves, how can you tell if they're dead or alive?"

47
00:02:55.553 --> 00:02:59.776
"Look for little brown lumps swelling along the branches.

48
00:03:00.908 --> 00:03:03.929
Then watch them after a warm spring rain and see what happens."

49
00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:06.490
Ben suddenly looked angry.

50
00:03:06.510 --> 00:03:09.431
"Why do you care so much about roses?

51
00:03:10.472 --> 00:03:11.512
Go away and play.

52
00:03:11.892 --> 00:03:13.133
I'm done talking for today."

53
00:03:14.914 --> 00:03:16.694
Mary skipped down the garden path.

54
00:03:17.975 --> 00:03:19.876
She liked Ben even when he was gruff.

55
00:03:21.076 --> 00:03:22.457
Suddenly she stopped skipping.

56
00:03:24.457 --> 00:03:27.919
A red-haired boy sat under a tree, playing a wooden pipe.

57
00:03:28.959 --> 00:03:30.360
But he wasn't the only one there.

58
00:03:31.688 --> 00:03:35.569
A brown squirrel was clinging to the trunk of the tree and watching him.

59
00:03:36.769 --> 00:03:39.150
A pheasant was peeping out from behind a bush.

60
00:03:41.551 --> 00:03:44.191
Two brown rabbits were inching closer to the boy.

61
00:03:45.352 --> 00:03:48.813
All the animals seemed to be listening intently to the music.

62
00:03:50.073 --> 00:03:51.913
"Shh," the boy said to Mary.

63
00:03:51.933 --> 00:03:54.614
"Stay quiet or you'll frighten them away."

64
00:03:55.974 --> 00:03:58.055
He finished playing and stood up slowly.

65
00:03:59.095 --> 00:04:02.992
The bird and the animals wandered away, but they didn't seem afraid.

66
00:04:03.012 --> 00:04:06.193
"I'm Dickon," said the boy.

67
00:04:06.213 --> 00:04:08.834
"And you must be Miss Mary.

68
00:04:09.374 --> 00:04:10.955
I have your seeds and your tools."

69
00:04:12.115 --> 00:04:14.256
He pointed to a brown package on the ground.

70
00:04:14.276 --> 00:04:18.397
"Let's sit down on this log and look at them," said Mary.

71
00:04:19.598 --> 00:04:20.998
Dickon unwrapped the package.

72
00:04:22.699 --> 00:04:26.960
He showed her a shovel, a rake, a hoe, a fork, and a trowel.

73
00:04:28.044 --> 00:04:31.586
There were also two packets of seeds for blue and white flowers.

74
00:04:31.606 --> 00:04:34.127
"I'll plant these for you myself.

75
00:04:34.648 --> 00:04:35.428
Where's your garden?"

76
00:04:36.569 --> 00:04:37.830
Mary didn't know what to say.

77
00:04:38.890 --> 00:04:40.671
Could she trust this strange boy?

78
00:04:40.691 --> 00:04:43.493
"Can you keep a secret?"

79
00:04:44.053 --> 00:04:45.294
she blurted out at last.

80
00:04:46.634 --> 00:04:47.495
Dickon looked puzzled.

81
00:04:47.515 --> 00:04:50.216
"I keep secrets from the other boys all the time.

82
00:04:51.457 --> 00:04:56.340
If I couldn't keep secrets about fox cubs and bird nests, nothing would be safe on the moor."

83
00:04:57.648 --> 00:04:58.869
Mary grabbed his sleeve.

84
00:04:58.889 --> 00:05:02.612
"I've stolen a garden," she said quickly.

85
00:05:02.632 --> 00:05:04.814
"Nobody wants it.

86
00:05:05.275 --> 00:05:06.355
Nobody cares for it.

87
00:05:06.916 --> 00:05:08.417
Nobody ever goes into it.

88
00:05:09.978 --> 00:05:12.160
Maybe everything is dead in it; I don't know.

89
00:05:13.361 --> 00:05:16.824
But they can't take it away from me when I care about it and nobody else does."

90
00:05:18.105 --> 00:05:19.727
And then she burst into tears.

91
00:05:21.008 --> 00:05:22.469
Dickon's eyes grew wide.

92
00:05:22.489 --> 00:05:24.711
"Where is this garden?"

93
00:05:25.131 --> 00:05:25.972
he said softly.

94
00:05:27.185 --> 00:05:28.471
Mary got up from the log.

95
00:05:28.491 --> 00:05:31.123
"Come with me, and I'll show you."
